h  that can actually enforce what they're  doing  canada's public safety minister would  not comment on calls for legislative  changes  at the end of the day the police even  investigate the police  i mean it is i hate to say it but it's  farcical  it's been going on like that for years  and he adds  too often it's the victim not the  officer who ultimately pays the price  jules knox global news kelowna bc  up next old wounds reopened after the  discovery of a suspected mass grave  why canada's dark chapter isn't over yet  flags are flying at half-mast across the  country for what is believed to be a  mass grave in bc  the chief of the tekemploops first  nation says the remains of as many as  215 children  were located on the property of an old  residential school  the practice of taking indigenous  children from their families is forever  a dark chapter in canada's history and  the deep pain  persists but as roslord reports there  are calls to move forward  with reconciliation if making sense of  it is impossible  canadians are at least trying to do  something about what happened at this  former residential school for indigenous  children  among the tributes across canada many  symbolize the number 215  the estimated number of children whose  remains have been discovered in  unmarked graves according to the tikkem  loop's first nation  flags are being lowered including at  ottawa's peace tower and at other  government  buildings there are calls for a national  day of mourning  from those who consider these tributes  only the beginning  we're going to most likely be bringing  them home which means  potentially unearthing them and going  through and identifying them  and then seeing which community they  belong to  so that's going to be so hard and so  horrendous  i felt grief you know  and and i felt a little bit  angry as well you know and i'm not an  angry person  by nature this first nations chief  suggests  only after a long period of reflection  has he  managed to find anything positive these  children's herd is gone  their pain is gone they're at peace  this is now about what we do to remember  this moment  but also how do we go forward from here  there are other questions how to fit the  discovery into the already disgraceful  history of residential schools  and into the pursuit of reconciliation  how to help our own children understand  it  especially indigenous students who live  with the repercussions  generations later as canadians look for  new ways and new symbols to honor the  children and their families  the full magnitude of the horror is  unknown  the tekem's first nation says  preliminary findings should be completed  by mid-june  it promises updates as they become  available ross lord  global news and that is global national  for this sunday night  i'm robin gill we leave you tonight with  more tributes from around the country  for the countless children lost in the  residential school system  thank you for watching donna friesen  will be at the anchor desk tomorrow  good night  you
 

